For Google DeepMind L6 / Senior Staff-level system-design preparation with 9+ years of Java backend experience, 
I would not start with “design Amazon” as one giant system.

For interview preparation, the better approach is to use an e-commerce system as a progressive system-design case study, 
where each stage introduces a deeper distributed-systems problem.

My recommendation:
      Use Amazon-like e-commerce as the primary system, but define the scope as:

              Design a large-scale e-commerce platform —>
                              Amazon/Flipkart-style —>
                                      supporting product discovery, 
                                      inventory, 
                                      cart, 
                                      order placement, 
                                      payment, and order fulfillment.


This is better than designing only eBay because it exposes more of the distributed-system problems that are valuable at L6.
================================================================================================================================================
Why this is the right system for your preparation:

            An e-commerce platform lets us cover almost the entire HLD interview landscape:

| Area                     | What we can learn                                     |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| API design               | REST APIs, idempotency, pagination                    |
| Scalability              | Horizontal scaling, sharding, partitioning            |
| Caching                  | Redis, cache-aside, invalidation                      |
| Database                 | SQL vs NoSQL, indexing, replication                   |
| Distributed systems      | Consistency, availability, partition tolerance        |
| Concurrency              | Inventory race conditions, overselling                |
| Messaging                | Kafka/event-driven architecture                       |
| Reliability              | Retry, timeout, circuit breaker                       |
| Transactions             | Order + inventory + payment                           |
| Distributed transactions | Saga, orchestration/choreography                      |
| Search                   | Elasticsearch/OpenSearch                              |
| Availability             | Multi-AZ / multi-region                               |
| Performance              | Latency, throughput, bottlenecks                      |
| Observability            | Metrics, logs, tracing                                |
| Security                 | Authentication, authorization, payment security       |
| Java/Spring              | Services, concurrency, WebClient, Kafka, transactions |
| System evolution         | Monolith → modular monolith → microservices           |

================================================================================================================================================
And most importantly, it gives us opportunities to answer the L6-level questions:

                  Why this design?
                  Why not another design?
                  What breaks at 10× scale?
                  What happens during failure?
                  What consistency is actually required?
                  Where is the bottleneck?
                  How would you evolve the system?

